WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.441
Permission-safe C starts with least
privilege and safe creation defaults.

2
00:00:04.521 --> 00:00:07.107
Request only the file access the operation
needs.

3
00:00:07.187 --> 00:00:10.081
Create files with permissions appropriate
to the data.

4
00:00:10.161 --> 00:00:12.747
Avoid broad access followed by attempted
repair.

5
00:00:12.827 --> 00:00:17.323
Treat permission-sensitive failures as
meaningful failures, not background noise.

6
00:00:17.403 --> 00:00:19.743
Protect both primary and secondary
outputs.

7
00:00:19.823 --> 00:00:22.470
Logs, reports, exports, caches, temporary
files,

8
00:00:22.550 --> 00:00:25.506
and diagnostics can all contain sensitive
information.

9
00:00:25.586 --> 00:00:28.726
Their permissions should follow their
content and purpose,

10
00:00:28.806 --> 00:00:31.516
not the fact that they are operational
side effects.

11
00:00:31.596 --> 00:00:35.537
Ownership, groups, directories, platform
behavior, inherited defaults,

12
00:00:35.617 --> 00:00:37.895
and process identity are part of the
design.

13
00:00:37.975 --> 00:00:40.746
The source code should not assume those
details away.

14
00:00:40.826 --> 00:00:42.550
It should document what matters,

15
00:00:42.630 --> 00:00:45.216
check what is important for sensitive
workflows,

16
00:00:45.296 --> 00:00:48.314
and fail safely when required properties
are not present.

17
00:00:48.394 --> 00:00:52.211
Avoid race-prone assumptions about paths,
metadata, and later file use.

18
00:00:52.291 --> 00:00:56.540
A check performed earlier may not describe
the object used later if the file system

19
00:00:56.620 --> 00:00:57.236
can change.

20
00:00:57.316 --> 00:01:01.626
Sensitive workflows should reduce that gap
and rely on platform-supported stable

21
00:01:01.706 --> 00:01:03.369
references where appropriate.

22
00:01:03.449 --> 00:01:04.557
Finally, use layers.

23
00:01:04.637 --> 00:01:08.640
Tests, warnings, static analysis, code
review, deployment documentation,

24
00:01:08.720 --> 00:01:13.153
and operational validation all support the
permission model from different angles.

25
00:01:13.233 --> 00:01:17.544
Secure file permissions are not only the
job of the system administrator or only the

26
00:01:17.624 --> 00:01:18.732
job of the developer.

27
00:01:18.812 --> 00:01:21.460
They are a shared part of responsible
engineering.
